## Summary
This multi-centre cohort study of 2571 women with PMOS/PCOS undergoing IVF found that preconception 1-hour plasma glucose during OGTT is a stronger and more consistent predictor of subsequent gestational diabetes mellitus than fasting or 2-hour glucose. The 1-hour glucose ≥11.6 mmol/l remained significant within the diabetic range, and the association was consistent across PMOS/PCOS phenotypes but varied by BMI. These findings support considering 1-hour glucose in preconception metabolic assessments for higher-risk women.

## Key Points
- Preconception 1-hour plasma glucose showed a stronger and more consistent association with gestational diabetes mellitus than fasting or 2-hour glucose in women with PMOS/PCOS undergoing IVF.
- Fasting, 1-hour, and 2-hour glucose levels were all significantly associated with GDM risk, but only 1-hour glucose ≥11.6 mmol/l remained significant within the diabetic range.
- The association of 1-hour glucose with GDM was significant in normal-weight and obese women, but not in overweight women.
- The associations were consistent across different PMOS/PCOS phenotypes.
- Findings suggest that preconception 1-hour glucose may add value in identifying women at higher risk of GDM among normal-weight and obese PMOS/PCOS women undergoing IVF.


